{
  "image_name": "59608559ff04b002dfe8903cd0d2fee5.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a designed interphase for solid-state lithium metal batteries and how it controls where lithium deposits during cycling. In the top schematic, a silver-containing carbon-rich interphase sits between the solid electrolyte and the current collector/substrate. During charging, lithium is deposited beneath this interphase, and some silver forms an Ag–Li alloy. After discharge and repeated cycling, the silver appears redistributed mainly toward the lower part of the interphase, creating a vertical gradient from top to bottom. This suggests the layer evolves into a structure that directs lithium plating near the bottom interface rather than randomly through the electrolyte contact.\n\nThe lower schematics explain the intended mechanism. A densified solid electrolyte is separated from the lithium anode by a porous, mixed-conductive but lithiophobic interphase and a thin lithiophilic layer near the lithium metal. Because the upper region has low electronic conductivity and is lithiophobic, lithium does not nucleate there. Instead, plating occurs reversibly at the bottom, next to the lithiophilic layer and Li anode, filling pores upward while avoiding a resistive interphase at the electrolyte side. On stripping, the structure remains void-free, indicating stable, reversible lithium plating and stripping.",
